# The mod-review workflow I’d use

You could later hand me one mod at a time and ask:

> “Analyze this Starsector faction mod and generate a Doctrine & Retrofit Pack compatible with the generator schema.”

I would output:

* faction strengths/weaknesses
* ship archetypes
* recommended native fits
* alternate retrofit roles
* poor-fit roles
* faction-specific hullmod tendencies
* weapon families
* capability gaps
* recommended foreign/installed gap fillers
* confidence/basis metadata
* structured JSON/TOML pack

That becomes a **review translated into machine-readable guidance**.

The nice part is that the program would still validate all of it against the actual installed version. If the mod updates, stale recommendations can be flagged instead of silently trusted.

## My recommendation

Build the system so **manual reviews are optional enrichment, not required input**.

The ideal hierarchy is:

```text
Raw mod scan
    ↓
Automatic faction capability analysis
    ↓
Automatic recommendations
    ↓
Optional knowledge pack
    ↓
Optional user overrides
```

That way you can install some obscure faction mod tomorrow and still get useful recommendations immediately, while a reviewed pack makes the advice sharper and more thematic.

I would definitely add this architecture before we freeze the planning docs, because it ties together faction analysis, retrofit recommendations, and future mod-specific review packs very cleanly.
